 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Shark NZ801UK anti hair wrap upright - suction has gone really weak over the last couple of weeks, barely picks up crumbs. Have emptied the bin. Any ideas before I buy a new one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Switch it off and unplug it (or take the battery off) first. You likely don't need a new one yet. Weak suction that develops gradually is almost always caused by airflow restriction rather than motor failure. Please check your pre-motor and HEPA filters first. If they are dirty, wash them in cold water only (no detergent) and let them dry completely for at least 24 hours. A damp filter will severely restrict airflow. Also, detach the hose and wand to ensure there isn't a blockage inside them or at the floorhead inlet. Let me know if you found any blockages or if the filters needed washing.\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — Lost suction is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage in the hose, tube or floor head — check and clear those before suspecting the motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok. Emptied the bin every time. I've never washed the filters, didn't know they were under the bin. Hose and wand look clear, coin goes through the hose fine. Should I wash the foam and felt ones?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, absolutely wash both the foam pre-filter and the felt/HEPA filter. Use cold water only with no detergent or washing-up liquid. After rinsing, shake off any excess water and leave them to dry completely for at least 24 hours in a warm, airy place. It is critical that they are bone-dry before you put them back in; a damp filter will severely restrict airflow and mimic a fault. Once they are fully dry, reassemble the vacuum and test the suction again."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Washed both and left them 24 hours. Massive difference, suction is back to like new. No burning smell or cut-outs at any point either. Thanks."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That is excellent news. It was most likely the clogged filters that were choking the suction; they are easy to miss but make a huge difference when clean. To keep it running like this, try washing them every three months or whenever you notice the suction starting to dip again. If anything else comes up with the vacuum, just let me know."
  }
 ],
